# What the SOURCE archive carries. ⚠ The wheel is governed by pyproject.toml;
# this file governs the sdist, and the two are separate lists.
#
# 🚨 WHY `prune tests` EXISTS, AND IT WAS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED. The sdist
# shipped 23 `tests/` members, and two of them are deliberately full of things a
# published artifact must not contain:
#
#   tests/test_devtools.py   planted KEY-SHAPED literals -- the fixtures that
#                            prove `scan-secrets` can actually fail
#   tests/test_archives.py   synthetic SRT cue blocks and WebVTT timings
#   tests/test_cache.py      the same
#
# So `python -m hato.dev scan-secrets dist/` and `scan-content dist/` both
# failed on the artifact -- correctly. They are not wrong; the archive was.
# ⛔ The fixtures are ours and synthetic, but a scanner cannot tell a planted
# fake from a real leak, and it should not have to: the answer is not to teach
# it exceptions, it is to stop shipping the suite.
#
# ⚠ Consequence, stated: the sdist cannot run its own tests. That is normal for
# a source distribution and harmless here -- the suite lives in the repository,
# which is hato's actual delivery route (spec/10-deployment.md §RULED).
prune tests

# ⛔ The dev runner's config. Pitfall P6 in full: the sibling project shipped
# its development config, the installed package READ it, and the headline call
# raised ConfigError for every user. It is already kept out of the wheel by
# pyproject.toml; this keeps it out of the sdist too.
exclude hato.config.json

# Working notes, run logs and the mutation engine are vault-only and are not
# published even to the repository -- but exclude them explicitly so a stray
# copy can never ride along in an archive.
prune _runs
prune mutants
prune gui-mock
exclude _mutants.mjs
exclude LEDGER.md LEDGER-HOT.md HANDOFF.md CONTEXT.md

# What the archive DOES carry beyond the package itself.
include LICENSE
include THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md
include README.md
include hato-run.cmd
# ⚠ The other two launchers, and one of them is load-bearing.
# `hato/startup.py` registers `hato-watch.pyw` BY NAME in a Windows Run key,
# so a source tree without it has a login entry pointing at nothing --
# silently, because a windowed interpreter has no console to say so.
include hato-watch.pyw
include hato-gui.cmd
# 🚨 GPL-3.0 CORRESPONDING SOURCE for the Windows standalone: §1 includes
# "the scripts used to control compilation and installation".
recursive-include packaging *.py
include packaging/hato.spec
recursive-include spec *.md
